Z||000013||olympic||12-14-1999||08:58 PM||||Years ago, my dad had a neat little roadside tire inflater. You would remove a spark plug and screw one end of it into the spark plug hole and attach the other end to the valve stem on the tire. Start the engine and it inflated the tire. It even had a pressure gauge on it. Anyone know the risks of running an air fuel mix in your radials ??? <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Thats probably the reason I haven't seen one of these lately.  ||||reg||3
Z||000014||Monkeyman||12-14-1999||09:46 PM||||Seems like the heat of the tire wouldn't be the best for gasoline, but what do I know.  That must have been before "Fix-a-Flat" in a can.  For $5US you can screw a can of this to your tire and, in minutes, be on your way again.  I'd hate to have to do all the work your dad did just to fix a flat.  BTW-wouldn't that hurt the motor?||||reg||1
Z||000015||olympic||12-14-1999||10:06 PM|||| Like I said, I haven't seen one of those contraptions in years. Probably since they came out with electric pumps that plug into the cigarette lighter and more recently the cans of fix a flat stuff. I'll guessing that these units were used in construction sites on heavy equipment where a spare tire wasn't available. BTW, Running a motor without the spark plug in one cylinder for thirty seconds won't hurt it. Everything is still getting lubricated and fuel wash-down would be minimal. ||||reg||10
Z||000016||Phil||12-14-1999||10:33 PM||||How about this old odd ball contraption? It was a slotted generator pulley for a VW Bug  so if your battery went dead you could wrap a rope around the pulley and pull start your car||||reg||1
Z||000017||batboy||12-14-1999||10:39 PM||||I hate to admit this, but when i was a teenager I had a 56 VW Beetle for a while. It had the rope starter pulley too.||||reg||1
Z||000018||Monkeyman||12-15-1999||01:41 AM||||Actually, that's not a bad idea.||||reg||1
Z||000019||DJRice||12-15-1999||09:09 AM||||Do any of you guys watch real TV?  I saw a video on Real TV last night of a hobby stock race where a guy slid into another car, and it broke his tire loose from the rim.  The tire continued to move at the same speed as the car and then re-wrapped itself around the rim.  Of course all of the air was gone, but it did end up back on the rim.  I got a kick out of that.||||reg||1
Z||000020||dogtired||12-15-1999||02:26 PM||||This is one of my FAVORITE threads   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Lets see Rope start my Lawnmower and VW, sounds like a Lawn-boy too me.<BR>Now for the tire inflators, I Have one, never used it but saw my grandfather use it many times on his tractors. 4cyl Farmall doesn't run too well on three cylinders.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><BR>Now for the inflator cans. Took a blown tire off a lawn tractor (not the ones above) went to tire store for replacement, The inside of the tire looked like the threads had come un-bonded form the inside surface. The tire man said that tire inflator in a can, the stuff that is suppose to stop the leak will degrade the rubber inside the tire, that is what is suppose to get in the hole and stop the leak. Anyway his comment was never use that stuff in a good tire cause it will cause it to go bad from the inside out. Anyway I try to not use that stuff, unless it is an emergency.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><BR>Just some more useless info.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||6
Z||000021||olympic||12-16-1999||02:52 PM||||I've heard that too about the "fix a flat" stuff. I think you're only suppose to use it to get your car to a tire shop and have the "fix a flat" washed out and the leak fixed properly. ||||reg||1
Z||000022||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||03:59 PM||||My wife looked on the forum (!I just know she's a closet Fiero fan, just too proud to admit it! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) and she said that she used the stuff for years and never had any problems.  I plan on putting it in a couple of tires that are flat with dryrot, so I don't care if it eats the tires up or not.  It'll probably be spring before I change the tires, so I'll let everyone know then.||||reg||1
